WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.768
Welcome.

00:00:01.048 --> 00:00:05.372
In this walkthrough, we will set up Scheme
on a working computer, open a real

00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:08.685
terminal in its browser, and connect from
a second desktop.

00:00:08.965 --> 00:00:13.461
The footage comes from two separate Ubuntu
desktop virtual machines, each with

00:00:13.461 --> 00:00:14.960
eight gigabytes of memory.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:19.144
They are actual computers with their own
operating systems and disks.

00:00:19.424 --> 00:00:22.475
Everything shown uses a fresh
demonstration account.

00:00:22.755 --> 00:00:25.464
You can pause whenever you need to copy a
command.

00:00:25.744 --> 00:00:29.873
The written guides beside this video
contain the commands and explain the

00:00:29.873 --> 00:00:30.608
alternatives.

00:00:31.158 --> 00:00:33.526
First, choose the computer that will do
the work.

00:00:33.806 --> 00:00:35.214
Scheme calls it the host.

00:00:35.494 --> 00:00:40.070
This is where your project files, terminal
sessions, and optional AI tools

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:40.379
live.

00:00:40.659 --> 00:00:44.073
Keep it powered on and awake while you are
working from another screen.

00:00:44.353 --> 00:00:48.643
The viewing device only needs a current
browser and a private way to reach the

00:00:48.643 --> 00:00:48.918
host.

00:00:49.198 --> 00:00:51.438
It does not need its own Scheme
installation.

00:00:51.718 --> 00:00:55.238
A monitor attached to the host is already
on the same computer.

00:00:55.518 --> 00:00:59.507
A separate laptop or phone needs the
connection we will set up later.

00:01:00.057 --> 00:01:02.404
Open the Terminal application on the host.

00:01:02.684 --> 00:01:05.969
On Ubuntu, the usual shortcut is Control,
Alt, T.

00:01:06.249 --> 00:01:10.251
Use the first command in the host guide to
update the package list, then

00:01:10.251 --> 00:01:12.308
install the small prerequisite tools.

00:01:12.588 --> 00:01:16.986
When sudo asks for your password, type
your normal computer password and press

00:01:16.986 --> 00:01:17.324
Enter.

00:01:17.604 --> 00:01:20.441
It is normal for the characters to remain
invisible.

00:01:20.721 --> 00:01:24.156
Tmux keeps the terminal sessions alive
when a browser leaves.

00:01:24.436 --> 00:01:27.252
The script utility supplies the terminal
connection.

00:01:27.532 --> 00:01:29.687
Git helps Scheme show project activity.

00:01:29.967 --> 00:01:31.972
Curl and unzip help with downloads.

00:01:32.252 --> 00:01:34.492
None of this requires a GitHub account.

00:01:35.042 --> 00:01:36.514
Now check the Node version.

00:01:36.794 --> 00:01:38.991
Scheme needs version twenty two or newer.

00:01:39.271 --> 00:01:42.749
If your computer already has that, you can
skip the installation.

00:01:43.029 --> 00:01:48.537
Otherwise, the guide uses the official nvm
installer, then loads nvm into this

00:01:48.537 --> 00:01:51.221
terminal and installs Node twenty two.

00:01:51.501 --> 00:01:53.655
Copy the commands in their displayed
order.

00:01:53.935 --> 00:01:55.727
Wait for each download to finish.

00:01:56.007 --> 00:01:58.269
Check the version again before continuing.

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:02.538
A new terminal may need to load nvm if its
command is not found.

00:02:02.818 --> 00:02:07.780
Scheme itself has no package dependencies
and no build step, so there is no

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:11.287
separate npm install to run inside the
Scheme folder.

00:02:11.837 --> 00:02:14.995
Open the public Scheme project page in the
host browser.

00:02:15.275 --> 00:02:18.283
The green Code button has a Download ZIP
option.

00:02:18.563 --> 00:02:21.720
You can also follow the direct ZIP link in
the guide.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.850
Extract the archive, then keep the
resulting Scheme folder somewhere

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:27.888
permanent, such as your home folder.

00:02:28.168 --> 00:02:30.344
Do not run it inside the archive viewer.

00:02:30.624 --> 00:02:34.805
Open the extracted folder in your file
manager and choose Open in Terminal.

00:02:35.085 --> 00:02:39.387
If that option is unavailable, open
Terminal yourself and change into the

00:02:39.387 --> 00:02:39.800
folder.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.664
Check that you can see the readme, the
server file, and the bin folder.

00:02:43.944 --> 00:02:46.035
Those tell you that you are in the right
place.

00:02:46.585 --> 00:02:50.848
Run the command that makes the launcher
files executable, then run Scheme

00:02:50.848 --> 00:02:51.257
Doctor.

00:02:51.537 --> 00:02:54.353
This check explains what is ready and what
is missing.

00:02:54.633 --> 00:02:57.705
Fix any missing required tools before you
continue.

00:02:57.985 --> 00:03:02.230
Claude Code, Codex, Ollama, and Tailscale
are optional at this point.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:05.027
You can make a normal Shell terminal
without them.

00:03:05.307 --> 00:03:07.227
Also check the character encoding.

00:03:07.507 --> 00:03:09.065
It should say UTF eight.

00:03:09.345 --> 00:03:11.798
The guide gives an Ubuntu fix if it does
not.

00:03:12.078 --> 00:03:16.062
If the doctor command says it cannot find
a file, check your current folder

00:03:16.062 --> 00:03:17.603
before reinstalling anything.

00:03:17.883 --> 00:03:21.211
A successful doctor check is a useful
first milestone.

00:03:21.761 --> 00:03:24.919
Start Scheme from its folder and keep that
terminal open.

00:03:25.199 --> 00:03:29.690
On the same working computer, open
localhost on port three thousand in your

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:30.169
browser.

00:03:30.449 --> 00:03:32.156
You should see Terminal and Connect.

00:03:32.436 --> 00:03:37.102
There is one detail for smaller computers:
the default memory reserve is eight

00:03:37.102 --> 00:03:38.239
thousand mebibytes.

00:03:38.519 --> 00:03:42.670
An eight gigabyte machine may have less
free memory than that, and Scheme will

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:43.788
refuse a new session.

00:03:44.068 --> 00:03:48.612
In this test, we use the documented
reserve of one thousand and twenty four.

00:03:48.892 --> 00:03:51.217
Set it in the terminal before starting
Scheme.

00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:55.959
This is a reserve check, not a memory
limit, and it does not make a large

00:03:55.959 --> 00:03:56.937
local model fit.

00:03:57.487 --> 00:04:01.263
Press New, choose Shell, and give the
session a name you will recognize.

00:04:01.543 --> 00:04:03.911
For the first test, your home folder is
fine.

00:04:04.191 --> 00:04:06.282
Start the session and wait for the prompt.

00:04:06.562 --> 00:04:10.039
Type the harmless hello command from the
guide and press Enter.

00:04:10.319 --> 00:04:14.398
Seeing Scheme is ready confirms that
typing in this browser reaches the

00:04:14.398 --> 00:04:15.375
working computer.

00:04:15.655 --> 00:04:20.034
You can open another tab for a different
task, rename a tab, or choose a

00:04:20.034 --> 00:04:20.946
project folder.

00:04:21.226 --> 00:04:25.774
Scheme can discover folders in common
places such as your projects or code

00:04:25.774 --> 00:04:26.389
directory.

00:04:26.669 --> 00:04:29.591
Only choose folders you intend the tool to
work with.

00:04:29.871 --> 00:04:32.965
The browser terminal has the access of
your host account.

00:04:33.515 --> 00:04:35.221
Now move to the second computer.

00:04:35.501 --> 00:04:39.576
We are using a private SSH tunnel for this
actual desktop test.

00:04:39.856 --> 00:04:43.845
Your host must already accept SSH
connections from this device.

00:04:44.125 --> 00:04:48.032
The Connect panel supplies a command for
your host, and the remote guide

00:04:48.032 --> 00:04:49.117
explains the pieces.

00:04:49.397 --> 00:04:52.405
Replace example names with your own host
and user.

00:04:52.685 --> 00:04:57.210
Run the tunnel command on the viewing
computer, keep that terminal open, and

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:00.365
open localhost on port three thousand in
its browser.

00:05:00.645 --> 00:05:03.397
Localhost now reaches the host through the
tunnel.

00:05:03.677 --> 00:05:07.779
If that port is already busy, choose a
different local port as the guide

00:05:07.779 --> 00:05:08.349
describes.

00:05:08.629 --> 00:05:12.939
We can see and type into the same host
session from this second desktop.

00:05:13.489 --> 00:05:17.805
For a phone, the guides recommend
Tailscale on both the host and the viewing

00:05:17.805 --> 00:05:18.203
device.

00:05:18.483 --> 00:05:23.049
Sign into your own account, then use
Tailscale Serve to give the local

00:05:23.049 --> 00:05:25.267
dashboard a private HTTPS address.

00:05:25.547 --> 00:05:27.531
Open that exact address on the phone.

00:05:27.811 --> 00:05:32.574
This part involves your account: our clean
desktop test does not claim to have

00:05:32.574 --> 00:05:36.238
completed your Tailscale sign in or tested
a physical phone.

00:05:36.518 --> 00:05:39.910
The smaller-screen preview shows the
layout and touch controls.

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:44.712
Use the touch key bar for keys such as
Escape and Control, and open Tools for

00:05:44.712 --> 00:05:46.121
the additional controls.

00:05:46.401 --> 00:05:50.776
Anyone who can access the dashboard can
use the host as your account, so keep

00:05:50.776 --> 00:05:52.139
that connection private.

00:05:52.689 --> 00:05:54.161
AI tools are optional.

00:05:54.441 --> 00:05:58.965
Install the tool you want on the host,
then start it once in a normal terminal

00:05:58.965 --> 00:06:00.415
and finish its own setup.

00:06:00.695 --> 00:06:04.108
Claude Code and Codex use your own access,
account, and billing.

00:06:04.388 --> 00:06:08.433
We checked their installation in this
clean environment; we did not sign into

00:06:08.433 --> 00:06:09.273
a cloud account.

00:06:09.553 --> 00:06:13.713
The local model option uses Claude Code
with Ollama on your machine.

00:06:13.993 --> 00:06:18.318
Choose a model that actually fits your
memory, download it, and configure the

00:06:18.318 --> 00:06:20.564
local model name before starting Scheme.

00:06:20.844 --> 00:06:25.250
A small model can prove the connection,
but it does not promise the quality of

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:26.775
a much larger coding model.

00:06:27.055 --> 00:06:31.491
The local launcher checks that the server
and model are available and refuses

00:06:31.491 --> 00:06:33.796
to quietly fall back to a cloud service.

00:06:34.346 --> 00:06:38.060
Once the first terminal works, you can
install the optional background

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:38.485
service.

00:06:38.765 --> 00:06:43.025
Keep any settings you need, including the
smaller memory reserve, exported in

00:06:43.025 --> 00:06:45.293
the terminal where you run the installer.

00:06:45.573 --> 00:06:49.373
Stop the foreground server, then run the
service installer from the Scheme

00:06:49.373 --> 00:06:49.733
folder.

00:06:50.013 --> 00:06:53.405
On Linux, the installer explains how the
user service starts.

00:06:53.685 --> 00:06:57.333
Follow its instructions if you want it to
start before you log in.

00:06:57.613 --> 00:07:01.967
We tested this by rebooting the host
desktop and checking that Scheme started

00:07:01.967 --> 00:07:02.306
again.

00:07:02.586 --> 00:07:04.975
The service keeps the web dashboard
available.

00:07:05.255 --> 00:07:08.967
It does not preserve a running command
through a power loss or a reboot.

00:07:09.517 --> 00:07:11.693
Here are three different recovery cases.

00:07:11.973 --> 00:07:14.725
Closing the browser leaves the host
terminal running.

00:07:15.005 --> 00:07:19.329
In our test, a job completed while the
viewing browser was fully closed, and

00:07:19.329 --> 00:07:21.491
its output was there when we returned.

00:07:21.771 --> 00:07:26.729
Restarting the Scheme web service also
kept that same terminal process and its

00:07:26.729 --> 00:07:28.064
unfinished job alive.

00:07:28.344 --> 00:07:30.072
Rebooting the host is different.

00:07:30.352 --> 00:07:34.500
The service returned, and saved tabs could
reopen, but the old running

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:35.685
processes were gone.

00:07:35.965 --> 00:07:40.297
If the connection stops working, first
check that the host is awake, Scheme is

00:07:40.297 --> 00:07:43.240
running, and your private connection is
still active.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:46.208
The troubleshooting guide follows that
same order.

00:07:46.758 --> 00:07:48.699
Before you finish, check four things.

00:07:48.979 --> 00:07:51.177
The doctor passes its required checks.

00:07:51.457 --> 00:07:53.739
A Shell command works in the host browser.

00:07:54.019 --> 00:07:57.966
Your viewing device reaches that same
session through a private connection.

00:07:58.246 --> 00:08:02.266
And you understand which tools or account
connections you still need to set

00:08:02.266 --> 00:08:02.427
up.

00:08:02.707 --> 00:08:06.771
The public guides cover configuration,
phones, remote desktops, and

00:08:06.771 --> 00:08:07.742
troubleshooting.

00:08:08.022 --> 00:08:11.542
Linux on these two Ubuntu ARM desktops was
tested.

00:08:11.822 --> 00:08:16.266
The written Mac and Windows through WSL
host paths still need separate

00:08:16.266 --> 00:08:17.091
verification.

00:08:17.371 --> 00:08:20.678
Start with one useful terminal, then add
the tools you need.

00:08:20.958 --> 00:08:23.625
Scheme is ready to become the screen you
return to.
